{{Life in Balisca}}

'''Social protection''' in [[Balisca]] (also known as '''social support'''), is very comprehensive compared to other countries. Since the late 1970s, Balisca has had one of the world's most advanced welfare systems, which guarantees a basic standard of living conditions for all [[Baliscans]]. Created almost entirely in the years following the [[1883 Confederal Convention]], the social security system was an outgrowth of the emerging belief that the {{W|minarchism|state's only role}} should be the ensuring of the well-being of its citizens, and would intervene benevolently and proactively regarding {{W|social safety net|social protection}}. 

Compared to that of other Latin American countries, Balisca's history is turbulent but not harsh enough to bar the country from following it's path of social development. According to social historians, Balisca's social development can be explained by [[W:Tobler's first law of geography|Tobler's first]] and [[W:Tobler's second law of geography|second laws]] of geography, which ultimately led to the gradual consolidation of a diverse and powerful {{W|proletariat}} in the country and had curtailed the dominance of the nobility and the subsequent formation of a powerful right wing. 

Social protection is almost entirely funded by taxes (to include {{W|Georgism|Georgist}} {{W|land value tax|land value}}, [[W:Gas tax|gas]], [[W:Carbon tax|carbon]] and {{W|corporate taxes}}), and executed typically by the [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] via [[W:Autonomous social centers|autonomous social centers]], sometimes with coordination at the [[Cantons of Balisca|cantonal-level]].

[[W:Horizontalidad|Horizontalidad]], and to a lesser extend {{W|Solidarity}} are strong values of the Baliscan Social Protection system, and is commonly comprehended in mutual aid, similar work, shared responsibility and common risks. 

Communal social programmes into those that guarantee income security and those that provide social and health services. Income security programmes come in two categories: social insurance, which provides income despite old age, illness, pregnancy, unemployment, or work-related injuries; and income security classified as welfare, which consists of transfers to aid families through measures such as child payments, maternity grants, payments to war victims and their survivors, financial support to those afflicted by disability, and [[W:Mutual aid (organizational theory)|mutual aid]] or pressing needs. Programs of the first category, income security guarantees, take some 60 percent of the funds expended for social welfare.

